159
00:16:41,320 --> 00:16:49,400
But like I answered with David's question, we do not need to have a very complex optical system in order to do holograms.

160
00:16:49,400 --> 00:17:09,480
Uh to do a hologram, the least amount of optics that you would need would just be one lens, which would take that laser beam and spread it out and give it enough area to cover the holographic plate with the object behind it, and that would be a single beam reflection hologram that you can view with light light.

161
00:17:09,480 --> 00:17:27,480
Uh there are other pieces of optical equipment that you can add to your system for more complex setups, uh things such as collimating mirrors, which takes the diverging wave front of that expanding laser light and puts it into a parallel beam.

162
00:17:27,800 --> 00:17:32,200
Also, there are uh spatial filters, which the light can go through.

163
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:37,000
It goes through an objective lens which focuses it down to a very small point.

164
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:44,520
And at that point in space, there is a pinhole, which is usually 10, twenty five microns in diameter.

165
00:17:44,520 --> 00:17:52,560
It's very tiny, and we pass that laser light right at that focal point through the pinhole in the spatial filter.

166
00:17:52,720 --> 00:17:54,880
That cleans up the profile of the beam.

167
00:17:54,880 --> 00:18:12,080
Anyone who's done amateur holography knows that a lot of times when you take a beam and you send it through a lens and you show that expanded beam on a white card, you'll notice that it's uh it's not it's pretty crappy looking sometimes if you're just using a lens.

168
00:18:12,240 --> 00:18:16,560
What a spatial filter does is it cleans that profile.

169
00:18:16,560 --> 00:18:34,720
It's actually a beautiful spread light, uh spread beam of laser light that uh focuses, uh not focuses, but uh shows up on the white card, and it uh really improves the overall quality of the holograms immensely when you use a spatial filter.

170
00:18:34,720 --> 00:18:37,360
But it's not necessary to do that.

